ffort to redo the wheel? Don't think so.\nAbsorbing all interaction with it within our Repo classes and not leaking it outside of them - that is worthwhile... is it a priority? I don't think so, but if someone does that part - I won't mind","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538140302,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTQzMDk2Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879#issuecomment-425430963"},"message":"@yarikoptic\n\u003e is it a priority? I don't think so, but if someone does that part - I won't mind\n\nThis is my thinking as well, but I'll add:  If someone initiates that, I will help, especially with the cases where I've used repo.git heavily (e.g., run/rerun, check_dates).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538147388,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTQ2NzIwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879#issuecomment-425467207"},"message":"\u003e Those usage patterns are what you found outside of or including our *Repo classes?\n\nCorrect.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538594119,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjc1OTEzNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879#issuecomment-426759137"},"message":"Eh, sorry, looking again at this, *many* of those `repo.git`s are mine :‑/ .  I wasn't aware of history like #1600.  Based on that, there seems to already have been agreement that gitpython should be avoided outside of `GitRepo`.\n\nI'm not quite sure how I came to the incorrect conclusion that `repo.git` use was preferred if there wasn't a fitting `GitRepo`, but I'm still confused by bits like \n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/b0cfdbb1b24a0a36fa48be6f7a2c68ed88d029a9/datalad/support/gitrepo.py#L1707-L1717\n\nPresumably that is stale and should be removed?\n\nAlso, if we're going to discourage gitpython calls, I think it'd be useful to have a public method that wraps `_git_custom_command` (or that simply is it) for one-off commands.   This could even be several methods that wrap common `_git_custom_command` patterns/outputs: boolean, str, list.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538594119,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzNjU4NTkyOA=="},"target":"2aed28be620e89d3d5b0e14f3b3cb7d395b269918bbe6c10974975ec849c0827","message":"Eh, sorry, looking again at this, *many* of those `repo.git`s are mine :‑/ .  I wasn't aware of history like #1600.  Based on that, there seems to already have been agreement that gitpython should be avoided outside of `GitRepo`.\n\nI'm not quite sure how I came to the incorrect conclusion that `repo.git` use was preferred if there wasn't a fitting `GitRepo`, but I'm still confused by bits like \n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/b0cfdbb1b24a0a36fa48be6f7a2c68ed88d029a9/datalad/support/gitrepo.py#L1707-L1717\n\nPresumably that is stale and should be removed?  (edit: or at least clarify that it's talking about use within `GitRepo`?)\n\nAlso, if we're going to discourage gitpython calls, I think it'd be useful to have a public method that wraps `_git_custom_command` (or that simply is it) for one-off commands.   This could even be several methods that wrap common `_git_custom_command` patterns/outputs: boolean, str, list.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1538638651,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjkxNTk4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879#issuecomment-426915984"},"message":"\u003e or at least clarify that it's talking about use within GitRepo? \nWell, originally I thought that was clear by the leading underscore. But yes, this should be clarified and the original intention was to allow for a quick first implementation of a method and then using `self.cmd_call_wrapper` (`GitRunner`) directly later on. But we clearly failed to follow this paradigm (or to take the second step to be more accurate). However, I think that's still, what we should aim for in order to clean that chaos and better deal with errors etc.\n\n\u003e I think it'd be useful to have a public method that wraps _git_custom_command (or that simply is it) for one-off commands\n\nActually, the reason for its existence is the opposite: If you need a git call this allows to quickly make this a method of `GitRepo` that is to be used in higher-level code, so you could care for high-level while not spending much time on proper low-level error handling etc., but still have it where it belongs and where it can be ironed out/safeguarded/etc. later on in a different PR and possibly by someone else.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1538638651,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzNjc3MTg3NQ=="},"target":"4c3eadcd009dd765c5b451e2c3bcd37f9ca8ba54dbccefd30a90a1027bc80a2a","message":"\u003e or at least clarify that it's talking about use within GitRepo? \n\nWell, originally I thought that was clear by the leading underscore. But yes, this should be clarified and the original intention was to allow for a quick first implementation of a method and then using `self.cmd_call_wrapper` (`GitRunner`) directly later on. But we clearly failed to follow this paradigm (or to take the second step to be more accurate). However, I think that's still, what we should aim for in order to clean that chaos and better deal with errors etc.\n\n\u003e I think it'd be useful to have a public method that wraps _git_custom_command (or that simply is it) for one-off commands\n\nActually, the reason for its existence is the opposite: If you need a git call this allows to quickly make this a method of `GitRepo` that is to be used in higher-level code, so you could care for high-level while not spending much time on proper low-level error handling etc., but still have it where it belongs and where it can be ironed out/safeguarded/etc. later on in a different PR and possibly by someone else.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538657511,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNzAwNjA0NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879#issuecomment-427006045"},"message":"Thanks, @bpoldrack\n\n\u003e Well, originally I thought that was clear by the leading underscore.\n\nSorry, my comment wasn't clear. With \"use\" above, I'm not talking about `_git_custom_command`, but about GitPython. Reading the \"without the need to figure out, how this is done via GitPython\" part, I'd think \"OK, so idealing I should try to use GitPython when creating GitRepo methods\". But it sounds like, while GitPython use outside GitRepo is the biggest concern, we also want to avoid GitPython **within** GitRepo methods, but to me this comment suggests it is the preferred way.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538657511,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzNjg4MjA5Mw=="},"target":"38e0d4ddbe4d0ced01440645a393e0d836b6a284e49b44fc4ecc27da9ab5d5fd","message":"Thanks, @bpoldrack\n\n\u003e Well, originally I thought that was clear by the leading underscore.\n\nSorry, my comment wasn't clear. With \"use\" above, I'm not talking about `_git_custom_command`, but about GitPython. Reading the \"without the need to figure out, how this is done via GitPython\" part, I'd think \"OK, so ideally I should try to use GitPython when creating GitRepo methods\". But it sounds like, while GitPython use outside GitRepo is the biggest concern, we also want to avoid GitPython **within** GitRepo methods, but to me this comment suggests it is the preferred way.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1538657616,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEzNjg4MjczNQ=="},"target":"38e0d4ddbe4d0ced01440645a393e0d836b6a284e49b44fc4ecc27da9ab5d5fd","message":"Thanks, @bpoldrack\n\n\u003e Well, originally I thought that was clear by the leading underscore.\n\nSorry, my comment wasn't clear. With \"use\" above, I'm not talking about `_git_custom_command`, but about GitPython. Reading the \"without the need to figure out, how this is done via GitPython\" part, I'd think \"OK, so ideally I should try to use GitPython when creating GitRepo methods\". But it sounds like, while GitPython use outside GitRepo is the biggest concern, we also want to avoid GitPython **within** GitRepo methods, but to me this comment suggests using GitPython is the preferred way.","files":null}]}